00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.460
"MARTHA"

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.580
The first thing that needs to be acknowledged about Suicide Squad is its troubled development.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.540
The specifics are hard to nail down because the people who actually know aren't going to say what happened

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.440
because they either need to play nice for the sake of their careers,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.140
or they're contractually restricted from ever mentioning it.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.880
But here's what we know:

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:26.220
David Ayer had only 6 weeks to bash out the script and then shot about 230 hours worth of footage

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.460
Basically trying to find the movie in the process of shooting it.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.980
At some point, Warner Brothers hires Trailer Park-

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.340
-The team that cut 'The Queen' trailer back in January-

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.020
-to do an alternate cut of the film, and test-screens both of them

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:41.400
However, audiences still didn't particularly like either cut,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.980
So a very expensive round of reshoots was scheduled for April, May, and a compromised THIRD cut -

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.660
-based principally on Ayer's version merged with the reshoots, and some ideas lifted from the Trailer Park cut

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:58.560
-is screened for audiences in June, with something fundamentally the same released to theaters in August.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.120
The result is more or less what you think it would be from that description.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.300
We're mainly going to be focusing, but not exclusively, on the film's bad editing.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.680
To really get into this, first, a primer

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.680
When people say a movie has "bad editing"

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.660
they're going to be mainly talking about two different kinds of editing:

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.000
Shot-to-shot

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.320
and scene-to-scene

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.240
Or Momentary editing,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.400
And Structural Editing

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.420
Momentary editing is the nitty-gritty editing:

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:31.280
What kinds of cuts are used, where are they placed, how far apart are they spaced, and how well is continuity preserved.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:37.420
Does a match edit actually work? Does intercut dialogue feel right? Can we follow the action properly?

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:38.860
And so on and so forth.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.360
Structural editng is the bigger picture:

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.020
What order are scenes in, how is the movie as a whole paced, when are flashbacks inserted,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.640
Are locations and geography properly established, are foreshadowing and callbacks properly implemented,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.680
and...all of that.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.080
Man of Steel is a good example for illustrating the difference

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.240
The Momentary editing in Man of Steel is generally okay,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.180
The editing problems are all structural;

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.840
with the flow from scene to scene being really poorly executed.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.720
Ideas and themes are abandoned, or strung out in the wrong order

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.760
And the overall package is really poorly paced.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.400
In equal measure, The Last Airbender is a great example of bad momentary editing.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.120
With lots of poorly selected cuts, bad reverse shots, and just a lot of terrible decisions all around.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:29.440
Suicide Squad, likewise, is so full of problems from structural to momentary,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.500
that I would seriously advise anyone with an interest in the art of cinematic editing do their own full autopsy

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.900
to see just how much went wrong and plain old doesn't work.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:50.280
Now whenever a studio starts to meld with a film, there's an instinctive response,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:57.580
a desire for a clean narrative that casts one side as the villains and the other as either the heroes or victims

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:03.340
The evil, greedy, heartless corporation beating up on the poor, misunderstood, visionary artist.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:10.740
Now there have been a few times in history where a potentially great film was ruined by a studio that either didn't get it

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.120
or maybe an executive had a personal beef with the director,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.420
or just something else happened that led to a chain of bad decisions.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.080
Most of the time, though, the underlying movie wasn't that good to begin with.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:29.160
Notoriously, Sony meddled heavily with Josh Trank's 2015 Fantastic Four film, Fanfourstic,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:35.080
ordering a round of heavy reshoots that, as a result of Kate Mara's awful wig, stand out like a sore thumb

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:42.900
The problem is that if you actually look at Fan4stic as a whole, the stuff that wasn't reshot isn't that good either.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:47.900
Trank's cut of the film was likely better in the sense that it was more cohesive,

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.980
but it wasn't a lost masterpiece.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.920
From Sony's perspective, they had a worst-case scenario on their hands:

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.780
Something that was neither broadly marketable, nor particularly good on its own merits.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.620
And this is the situation that Suicide Squad was almost certainly sitting in.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.340
The unfocused production resulting in a movie too grim and moody for mass appeal,

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:14.380
while being too...loose and meandering to draw an audience through critical acclaim and positive word of mouth.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.300
Like Fan4stic, The earlier cuts of the film were likely more coherent,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:25.440
but would've been paced even worse and still plagued with various plot incoherencies.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.400
So there's an element in Suicide Squad

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.020
It-it's a tiny thing

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.740
An insignificant thing in the scope of it all,

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:45.300
but I'm going to start with it because it's emblematic of the kind of problems that the movie has as a pretty direct result of it's chaotic development.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.020
There's this really old standby rule in screenwriting:

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.240
The Rule of Threes:

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.320
You set up an idea, you remind the audience, then you pay it off

00:07:53.500 --> 00:08:00.860
It's a rule because it's a really effective way of keeping the audience informed about smaller details that the characters would be familiar with

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.720
so that when they come into play, they don't feel like they're coming out of nowhere.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.620
So it really stands out when Suicide Squad manages to screw it up

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:12.320
even though they were sort-of clearly using it?

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.380
Alright: Captain Boomerang has this pink unicorn

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:20.920
It's first introduced as a gag during his titlecard where it flashes, "Fetish: Pink Unicorns"

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.600
Leaving aside the sexual implications,

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.400
during the gearing up segments, he pulls a pink unicorn stuffed animal out of his duffel bag

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.220
and covertly tucks it inside his jacket.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.040
So that right there is the setup

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:36.260
We're shown a prop, and we're shown exactly where it's put:

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.780
Inside his jacket, left side.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:40.620
It's a very deliberate action.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.140
Moving forward to the first big action scene,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:48.540
Boomerang gets knocked around a bit, fights a bit, and the stuffie flies out of his jacket during the melee.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:53.480
And afterwards, he again, covertly stuffs it into his jacket, left side

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.360
Again, super deliberate.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:56.720
There's the reminder.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.280
Then, in the tower brawl, Captain Boomerang is wrestling with a tar monster who stabs him right in the heart

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:04.160
And, for a second, you go "Oh no!"

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:09.240
But, being a savvy and attentive viewer, you immediately go "Ah, wait! The unicorn."

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:12.480
And you pat yourself on the back as Boomerang pulls out the knife...

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.520
...stuck in a wad of money.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.540
The unicorn, incidentally, is never seen again after the reminder.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:25.240
Now, the apologetic reaction is to assume that this is intentional.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:29.700
That it's deliberate misdirection, a subversion of expectations.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.380
You're expecting the unicorn, but are surprised when it's not the unicorn.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:41.400
However, if you were trying to subvert the expectation,  you would still want to show the unicorn at the moment of subversion

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.060
So, what happened?

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:49.120
Well, maybe the unicorn was orignally a wad of bills, but one day Jai Courtney decided a unicorn was funnier,

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.460
but they'd already shot the office brawl a month earlier and no one caught it in the chaos.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.440
Well maybe there was a payoff for the unicorn but it hit the cutting room floor.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.280
Well maybe the unicorn was never meant to pay off, and the wad of cash was better established,

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:08.020
but later edits biased towards the unicorn because its funnier, and establishing the cash hit the cutting room floor.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.700
Like, maybe amidst Boomerang's looting he finds the cash and stashes it in his vest or something

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.940
but we never saw that in the finished cut.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.740
Either way, it's a pretty bad edit for something really minor

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.800
And it's a microcosm of the problems with this film:

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:25.440
How the edit does a poor job of keeping track of all the balls that are  in the air,

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.200
how it foreshadows things that never pay off,

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.000
and how its plagued with extraneous elements that exist only as set up for future films.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:44.900
Swinging to the other end of the spectrum is an element that the movie actually does establish well and clearly,

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:46.400
and then just ruins.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.300
So the antagonist of the movie is 'The Enchantress'

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.980
An ancient witch who's weakness is her heart:

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:56.080
this wicker-looking thing that Waller keeps in a box strapped to a bomb

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.220
So we're told about the heart,

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.860
"Some say the witch has a secret buried heart,

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:03.140
and whoever finds it can control the witch."

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.620
Then the functionality of the heart is demonstrated

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:09.580
[screams]

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:11.200
Then it's reinforced,

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:15.120
[speaks foreign language]

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.160
[foreign language]

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.620
It's the raising of the stakes when Enchantress gets her heart back

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:27.540
And going into the final fight, Rick Flag lays out the plan:

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.440
"You got a move here Flagg?"

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.640
"We gotta cut her heart out!"

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:40.560
And just in case you weren't aware of the jeopardy, the visual design of Enchantress reminds you constantly by giving her a visibly glowing green heart.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.260
This is, at this point, INCREDIBLY well-established

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:47.220
It's the single best established rule of the films fiction

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:54.780
If there were anything in this movie, that you could feel 100% confident trusting the audience to follow along in a chaotic moment,

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:55.960
this is it:

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.920
The Enchantress' weakness is her heart.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.260
And then THIS happens

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.240
"You messed with my friends!"

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:11.880
"Her hearts out, we can end this!"

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.560
"Her heart's out, we can end this!"

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.560
[distorted] "HER HEARTS OUT"

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:23.520
So all the momentum, both emotional and kinetic, the flow of action across the screen is stopped dead,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.940
So that Flagg can reexplain what's going on.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.420
Then, just to keep it awful,

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.380
the edit completely loses track of where Enchantress is.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:39.620
She doesn't come back onscreen until this super-close -up shot that does nothing to help orient where she is in the room,

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:44.620
We cut before she fell down, so from the point of view of the rest of these slow-mo shots,

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.320
she just kind-of...vanishes

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.640
and Harley, who was standing right next to her, isn't anymore

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:50.460
And, just..

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:51.660
Okay?

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:52.980
Oh, and then the heart!

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.760
Harley pulls the heart out with her hand,

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.280
Rips it right out, full-fist!

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.020
And then it's just -poof!- gone

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.380
until Boomerang picks it up under a bench after everything's over

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:08.980
Normally a detail like that wouldn't matter, except it's the object that the jeopardy revolves around.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.120
Now editing is a process of decision making.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:13.540
What do you show?

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.000
What do you NOT show?

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.420
And it's often a zero-sum game,

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:24.180
with huge opportunity-costs because you're working within relatively small chunks of time in order to communicate everything vital

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.240
while still managing making the timing feel -

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:27.380
and here's a big word:

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.300
verisimilitudinous.

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:31.220
That doesn't mean real,

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.120
That means a plausible simulation of real

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.320
You know it's fake, but it feels acceptable.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.160
You can buy in, you can suspend disbelief.

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:48.480
So, that opportunity cost, they either didn't shoot or chose to cut Enchantress falling down, Harley throwing away the heart -

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.600
Anything that would help keep track of the action,

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:52.340
but...

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:58.940
they included a re-explanation of the one thing the audience should thoroughly understand.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.280
[distorted] HER HEARTS OUT

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.140
That right there, is bad editing.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:13.240
Alright, so this one is just a singularly awful edit.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.700
"-games with you man.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.640
It's not real!"

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.200
"He's right. It's not real."

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.500
There's no motion to lead into the cut,

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.760
We're focused on the wrong character leading into the cut,

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:29.280
And the crew is already way too far into the action on the far side of the cut.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:34.940
In a moment where they're trying to build up momentum and action, they end up skipping too much time and space

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.900
The difference between the two sides of the edit are too discontinuous,

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.840
making the edit feel jarring in a moment where it should feel fluid.

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:48.920
This is honestly the kind of edit you'd expect to see from a mediocre film student, or, well.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:50.480
A trailer house.

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:59.000
Speaking of things that feel like they should be cut by a trailer house

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.680
the opening seconds of a movie are so critical.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:03.280
I mean -

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.260
I-I shouldn't even need to explain that

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:11.060
There's a littany of terrible movies out there that at least managed to get their opening few seconds right,

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.380
And Suicide Squad doesn't even manage that.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:20.320
The opening shots of the film live in the void between punchy, fast-paced cuts and slow, deliberate cuts.

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:24.440
They don't hang out long enough for you to really take in what they're trying to tell you,

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:29.640
but they're not fast enough to blur together into a single conceptual image.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:36.120
This is a problem that's aggravated by the fact that the movie throws up a lot of hard-to-read-text in the first few seconds

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.780
Like, let's look at this opening shot:

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:44.860
This is actually an example of how a bunch of good ideas can be layered together to create a single bad idea

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:51.600
This transition is really three different transitions that, combined, turn the opening seconds into visual soup.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:56.020
We transition into the shot through the slit in the rotating DC Comics logo,

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:00.020
so right off the bat we got lines all pulling our focus right to the horizon.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:04.880
which... works with the first frames being the dark blob of terrain in the middle of the shot.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:05.860
because

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.740
on TOP of that transition we also have a focus racks.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:15.540
so it actually takes a handful of frames for things to snap into focus for your eyes can really grab something.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.480
And then, on top of that, is this surreal, neon-color grade,

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:26.280
which, itself, takes a few more frames to transition into the awful shades of concrete that is the films' normal color grade.

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.540
So that's a really visually busy first two seconds.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:33.820
And, in the midst of that, they toss up location titles in the lower right.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:35.760
And, before you have a chance to read it -

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:42.860
-because your eyes were almost certainly focused on this tower and these two white balls as the primary objects of interest in the frame-

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:45.520
-the camera starts flying over dark grass.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:52.460
So, now you have black text in a hard-to-read font flying over noisy, dark, dark, green grass,

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.320
And then it's gone because we immediately cut to this:

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:01.260
Now, your eyes, which were down in the lower right, trying to read the location, are first going to reset to the middle of the frame

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:06.280
Both out of instinct, and because there's a skull, and the brain is hard-wired to seek faces.

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:10.500
Next priority is all the text, but first a truck drives in front of everything,

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:14.480
Then soldiers walk past the camera, then you actually get a chance to read,

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:18.300
and around the time that you're getting frustrated by this half-demolished word,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:19.980
we cut into the guard post.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:23.480
And you've got about two seconds to absorb any pertinent information,

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:25.700
before we cut to inside Deadshot's cell.

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:30.400
And it was at this point I knew I was in for an editing nightmare.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:35.420
There's a phenomenon in film called 'The Kuleshov Effect,' which we could talk about in length

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:42.520
but the underlying idea is that more meaning is created from the interaction of two shots than of a single shot in isolation.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.700
We can already see this in the movie:

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:49.200
We start flying over the water towards the compound, and then cut to the writing on the wall,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:53.680
and assume that this wall is a detail of the building from the previous shot.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:58.660
And then we cut to the guard room, which we assume is inside the building we were just looking at,

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:03.000
and then it falls apart, in a very small way when we go inside the cell.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.580
This guard is sitting here, watching these monitors

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:10.020
We're instinctively going to follow, or try to follow this person's eyeline.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:15.880
But, rather than cutting to an angle that reflect's the guard's perspective via the security cameras,

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:20.940
we're instead much lower to the ground, looking up at Deadshot working his punching bag.

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:26.600
It's a cut that's....technically correct, in that we're moving deeper into the facility with each cut

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:28.240
But, it's off.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:33.060
It's the cinematic version of putting the emfases on the wrong sillabbles.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:39.000
And here's the thing: they use this exact technique that I'm describing in reverse when the next scene with Harley ends.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:42.560
Heck, they use it again at the end of the movie

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:46.860
They're clearly not trying out some methodical avant-garde style of editing,

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:48.420
It's - it's just bad.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:58.140
So the first 20 minutes of the film are consumed by a bad musical montage

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:02.200
It's also highly redundant and full of dead ends

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:08.220
Now Suicide Squad has a big cast - it's an ensemble piece with like, eight core characters.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:13.540
It's a lot of people to introduce with the added complexity of explaining what their comic book gimmick is,

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:16.020
and maneuvering them into place within the plot.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:25.720
Further complicated by the fact that the underlying conceit of the film is that the cast are all antisocial villains working for the government more or less against their will.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.620
That's a lot to set up.

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:32.640
Now they introduce almost everyone with capsule sized flashbacks narrated by Waller,

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:33.360
which....

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.260
...is a pretty bad way of introducing your protagonists

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:39.360
Suicide Squad, like ogres, has layers,

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.520
except in this case its layers of bad, awkward decisions.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:46.440
So the bad musical montage is bad because it's cut like a movie trailer.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:50.660
It's all sizzle at a time where the film should be busy establishing themes and conflict.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.080
As with many things we've already addressed,

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:56.180
what they're doing here isn't 100 percent wrong,

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.480
It's not an idea that's rotten to the core, it's just incredibly inefficient.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:07.920
Cutting your opening for sizzle isn't a bad idea until it's gone on for 30 minutes and you're just starting to get around to the actual plot.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.780
The original movie would've been much longer, and much slower paced

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:17.100
but by cutting the introductions for sizzle, trying to turn it all into a high-energy peppy montage,

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:23.460
that inefficiency stands out because they keep needing to come back to the introductions over and over again.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:29.180
To the point that halfway through the movie, they're JUST finishing introducing the first four characters.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:52.140
So where a five to eight minute scene with Deadshot could easily fit in all the pertinent details plus some,

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:56.800
trying to do it all in poppy trailer-style means that they need to keep coming back

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.960
Which in turn means there's no time for anyone else,

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:07.280
putting the story in a place where the core conceit doesn't work because they were only able to squeeze in negotiations with Deadshot and no one else.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:09.700
And I haven't even addressed the soundtrack yet

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:15.240
This trailer-style peppy montage relies heavily on pop music in the bluntest way possible.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.180
It's a prison in Louisiana!

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:19.420
'House of the Rising Sun' by the Animals!

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.360
Harley isn't your plaything,

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:23.340
'You Don't Own Me,' by Lesley Gore

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:27.640
Amanda Waller is bad and she's going to introduce a bunch of devils

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:28.920
'Sympathy for the Devil!'

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.540
Harley's a superfreak, so

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:32.060
Let's just play 'Superfreak.'

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:34.300
An Aussie? Who does dirty deeds?

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:36.460
'Dirty Deeds Done Dirt Cheap.'

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:38.400
El Diablo lost control and he's

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:40.860
♪ 'Slippin into Darkness!'♪

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.680
Jesus, this writes itself.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.920
Killer Croc is unfortunate so, hey!

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:46.240
'Fortunate Son!'

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:49.260
Will Smith is black and he shaved his head!

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:50.500
'Black Skinhead!'

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.040
They're assembling? Like an army?

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:54.360
'Seven Nation Army'

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:56.000
They're flying in a helicopter?

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:57.380
'Spirit in the Sky!'

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:01.760
Like, any one of these on it's own would be blunt, but appropriate.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:06.440
However, when it's you're one trick that you use over, and over again?

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:08.900
Oh yeah, and that wasn't even all of them.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:12.600
In fact, from the opening shot to the title card, it's end to end.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:17.260
They've already used three different songs before the title even comes up.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.300
Baz Luhrmann has more restraint than this!!

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:30.860
On repeat viewings, the first half of this movie becomes almost unbearable

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:38.640
because you already know how it ends, so you spend the first 50 minutes sitting through numerous threads that you know don't matter

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:41.420
One of the most obvious is this guy, Griggs

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.000
Griggs is one of the head guards at Belle Reve,

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:49.100
And the first three minutes of the movie aren't spent introducing us to Deadshot and Harley,

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:53.020
they're spent showing us just how awful Griggs is.

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:56.500
Not Belle Reve, not being locked up in general,

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:58.380
Griggs in specific.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:01.500
Now if they wanted him to be a bit of comic relief, that's fine

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:05.060
In fact, if there's one thing that this movie actually really needed,

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:10.440
it was a lot more...meh...antagonistic rapport between the squad and the rest of the world

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.900
So our fist bit is this back and forth between Griggs and Deadshot

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:16.300
which could've built up to that kind of a beat:

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:18.720
Grigg ribs Deadshot, Deadshot ribbs back,

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.900
they both laugh, call each other an asshole, walk away.

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:27.240
Instead... Griggs has Deadshot beat with batons by a dozen guards.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.740
Next up is Harley - and it's the same deal.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:31.280
This could be banter

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:34.040
Antagonistic banter, but still banter.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:40.360
Instead, the scene cuts to a flashback of Griggs outright abusing and humiliating her.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:43.040
These are the opening scenes of the movie.

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:45.620
This is the pace and tone being set.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:51.180
They're clearly setting up a direct obvious antagonism between the squad and Griggs.

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:56.400
Through that first 40 minutes they keep reinforcing that antagonism and upping the stakes:

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:00.960
Foreshadowing future resolution to the point that Harley outright tells Griggs,

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.300
"You're SO screwed"

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:04.440
"What do you mean by that?" [Harley laughs]

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.780
"What do you mean by that?"
[Harley continues laughing] "Get off, me, get off!"

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:08.140
"Harley what do you mean by that?!?"

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:11.200
And...that's the last we see of Griggs.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:15.500
He doesn't even show up at the end of the movie when the squad are back in prison.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.140
Now, there's a couple different problems going on here.

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:19.800
The first is a scripting problem,

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:23.480
Griggs was overdeveloped in the first half of the movie and then dropped.

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.640
The second, is an editing problem.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:30.540
Because of that lack of payoff, Griggs should have been significantly trimmed down

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:38.140
Well, there's another scripting problem, which is that the staff at Belle Reve are so consistently absurdly abusive to the squad,

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:42.420
that it strains the credulity when it comes to the plan of recruiting them to do missions.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:49.040
I mean, these aren't people who are going to be cooperative and useful, because you've treated them like absolute garbage

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:53.360
In turn, this doesn't make Waller look like a master tactician

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:58.960
Someone who is, as she claims, "the best at getting people to act against their own self-interests,"

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:01.620
It-it just makes her look like an idiot.

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:04.980
"Psychotic anti-social freaks - it makes no sense."

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:15.020
So a twist is a surprise subversion of the audience's expectation.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:21.320
But, in order for a twist to work, expectations need to be established before they can be subverted.

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:29.300
So there's a twist in the middle of the movie where Boomerang tricks Slipknot into trying to escape and Flagg blows up the bomb in Slipknot's neck

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:35.200
The twist doesn't work though because Slipknot was clearly introduced as disposable.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:42.220
Everyone else in this squad is introduced with the heavy-handed soundtrack choices, name cards, logos and a bio from Waller.

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:43.520
It's bad,

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:45.660
but it still sets a precedent.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:49.900
So when Slipknot steps out of an SUV and gets a one-line introduction,

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:54.020
"Here comes Slipknot, the man who can climb anything. Wonderful."
[Seven Nation Army plays in the background]

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:58.840
It's a pretty clear tell to the audience that he's not important and you shouldn't care.

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.820
So when he dies, it doesn't mean anything.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:03.580
It's....

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:05.460
Who cares?

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:06.960
You were told not to care.

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:16.380
I suppose we should take some time to talk about the actual antagonist of the film, Enchantress

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:21.600
who's possessing the body of June Moon, who's in love with Rick Flagg.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:25.540
Now there's a lot of problems here and this is going back to the underdeveloped script,

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:32.500
I mean, the biggest that she's being an absurdly powerful opponent for a team that should be busy doing...like, a heist or something

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:39.820
I mean this speaks to the basic fiction of the movie, that Waller's putting together a task force because.....what if,

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:48.940
"What if Superman had decided to fly down, rip off the roof of the White House and grab the President of the United States right out of the Oval Office?

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:50.900
Who would've stopped him?"

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:53.560
But if that's the case why is Captain Boomerang here?

00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:55.120
Or Harley Quinn?

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:56.320
Or Killer Croc?

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:57.400
Or Slipknot?

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:02.780
Like, sure, Killer Croc is a big guy and he's scary, and he can take a punch,

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:06.600
but at the end of the day, he's a linebacker with a skin condition.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:11.160
He's not superhumanly strong, he's just...regular strong.

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:14.880
Boomerang isn't even special. He's just a thief with a gimmick.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:18.040
Harley? Harley is pure liability.

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:19.160
Anyway-okay-So-

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:24.840
Enchantress is introduced as part of the team, and then her powers demonstrated to the brass at the Pentagon,

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:31.860
And then she more or less immediately betrays Waller and releases her brother in Midway City as part of her plan to escape

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.480
But, at the same time, in between those points,

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.360
And this is speaking directly to the poor structure,

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:42.920
There's the demonstration at the Pentagon, followed by a second montage of introductions to the Squad

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:50.280
None of which move the plot forward at all because the parts where Waller and Flagg try to negotiate with Croc, Boomerang and Harley were all cut,

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:56.920
And Diablo just tells them to piss off, so the only one they actually get to negotiate with is Deadshot.

00:27:56.920 --> 00:28:03.000
But, that doesn't go anywhere either, because they then immediately ignore the entire conversation, so...

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:04.700
Okay?

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:08.100
The only one that tells us anything new is the conversation with Diablo.

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:13.220
Which builds his character a bit, shows the audience that he's basically at peace with his punishment,

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:18.720
So that's useful character development, even if the structure of the scene does nothing to progress the movie itself.

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:24.040
Also, keeping in mind that the actual plot hasn't started yet.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:29.700
Oh, also they bring June Moon to the prison and highlight her presence with a close-up reaction shot, as they arrive

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:38.540
But she never interacts with any of the rest of the Squad, so when she goes rogue, no one on the team except Flagg has any emotional stake in the situation.

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:44.240
Anyway, all of these largely pointless meetings culminate in Flagg telling Waller that it's a dumb plan

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:50.780
because they're mostly worthless and their hostility just makes them a liability in any operation where loyalty would be an asset.

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:53.740
Which is every operation ever.

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:58.580
The scene ends with this ominous line where Flagg asks Waller what she's really up to

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:00.180
And Waller tells him

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:03.460
"Its a need-to-know and all you need to know is you work for me."

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:09.200
Now, that's actually important, because it tells the audience that she is, in fact, up to something.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:11.160
It's foreshadowing a reveal.

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:17.300
Waller has secret plans, that are at odds with the interests of either her bosses, or the Squad, or both.

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:20.280
Don't worry though, we don't find out what they are.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:24.300
Because she doesn't actually have secret plans.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:27.680
At least not as far as this movie is concerned.

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:28.680
So....

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:31.400
Yeah?

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:35.640
Then there's a couple scenes with the Joker and he starts to orchestrate Harley's break-out...

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:40.940
And then we finally get the plot rolling with Enchantress releasing her brother in Midway City.

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:43.040
And - oh yeah! Her brother!

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:47.240
So, if this feels like it comes out of left field half an hour into the movie,

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:51.260
that's because it more or less comes out of left field a half hour into the movie.

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:56.220
Now, prior to this, Enchantress' brother Incubus is only mentioned in her title card

00:29:56.220 --> 00:30:01.860
which were all written by Trailer Park for comedy, and freeze-frame easter eggs for the home audience.

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:04.380
Like, Flagg's list his golf handicap.

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:06.380
The point is these are jokes.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:11.940
The next glancing reference is Enchantress looking at this page, during the demonstration at the Pentagon.

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:18.160
Except, when the camera is looking at this page, Waller is talking about Enchantress and telling the room

00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:21.320
"Everything we know about her is in your briefing packs."

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:28.880
So the relevance of this shot, that it represents some potential jeopardy, really isn't clear until you're already familiar with the plot.

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:34.060
The visual difference, between what we're being shown, and what we've been shown in the past is nominal.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:37.640
No one's mentioned that there's a second statue that poses a similar risk

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:41.220
and Waller is summing up information that we're already familiar with,

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:48.140
All of the film's signifiers are telling us that 'this is old information, we don't need to care.'

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:54.740
So, when Enchantress is snooping around Waller's apartment and finds her brother's statue sitting on the shelf like a tchotchke,

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:56.940
It....comes out of nowhere.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:03.800
And this whole 'betrayal' culminates in one of the best worst moments in the movie.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:11.340
So, from the audience's perspective, Incubus is wrecking Midway City and we know that Flagg knows that Enchantress was up to no good.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:17.180
He doesn't know exactly what she did, but, presumably, he can put two and two together.

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:24.540
Well, he clearly doesn't, and he doesn't even bother to tell his boss that maybe we can't trust the witch right now, because she might be responsible

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:26.380
But, anyway, putting that aside,

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:32.840
Flagg and Moon are suddenly in the subway under Incubus, and he berates her into transforming, and..

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:34.380
[whispers] "Enchantress"

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:36.800
"Flagg, talk to me, what's going on down there?"
[phone rings]

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:39.580
"Amanda, she bolted."

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:40.840
"Say it again?!"

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:42.460
"She bolted!"

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:45.680
Okay, so, this is an important moment:

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:49.820
This is the moment that Flagg realizes he's made a big mistake

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:53.380
And it's the moment that really kicks the plot into high gear,

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:56.780
and instead of using that to build tension,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.000
Hard cut, and we learned what happened,

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:01.620
Over the radio.

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:06.420
A masterpiece of bad decisions.

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:13.740
Coming back to the subject of twists,

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:16.600
This movie actually has a twist, I guess?

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:17.440
But...

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:19.160
It's bad.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:20.920
Surprise.

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:23.920
After the squad finally gets assembled, at the midpoint of the movie,

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:28.300
Waller gives them their mission, which is rescue HVT-1, which...

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:34.220
I mean, wasn't the whole point of Task Force X to throw them at metahumans that are wrecking stuff?

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:43.160
"What if Superman had decided to fly down, rip off the roof of the White House, and grab the President of the United States right out of the Oval Office?"

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:45.780
"NY City's under attack. It's nonhuman entity."

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:47.780
"I take it you know what to do, sir."

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:49.600
"Activate Task Force X.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:52.480
Get Amanda Waller and her whole circus on scene ASAP"

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:56.480
Okay so that's confusing in it's own right but they eventually get to HVT-1

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:59.300
And it turns out it's...Waller...

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:02.200
She was in Midway City this whole time...

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:06.840
So this is a twist that isn't actually a twist because it's pointless.

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:10.900
Nothing changes by hiding that information,

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:15.100
Nothing changes when you find out that Waller is the target.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:19.360
Waller being in the city doesn't make sense in the first place,

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:24.140
and..and then Waller kills  her staff and says,

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:24.920
"What?

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:27.440
They weren't cleared for any of this. Any of it."

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:30.080
Well, then-then who were they?

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:31.560
What's going on?

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:33.140
Why are you here?

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:35.160
Why were they here?

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.820
What are you ACTUALLY doing?

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:45.300
This is one of those moments that exists to try and surprise the audience, but it just raises more questions than it could ever answer

00:33:45.340 --> 00:33:49.660
And they're bad questions, it's not enticing questions it's...

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.480
...questions of confusion.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:34:00.440
There's-there's a lot more that I havent even touched on.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:03.560
Like how the Joker's plot culdesac is used,

00:34:03.560 --> 00:34:08.280
How Harley's story was gutted because it was an endless downer that still didn't pay off

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:13.400
How the wrap up doesn't even mention that Diablo sacrificed himself for the squad,

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:18.060
But instead I'll just rattle those off in list form to give you the idea that they're there.

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:26.560
This, I think, is a pretty good vertical slice of the kind of editing problems that plague Suicide Squad from beginning to end.

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:28.480
2016.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:34.820
[bottle drops on floor]

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:47.360
[slightly upbeat music]

